<%#
  The project's task checklist.

  `id="project-tasks"` IS THE TURBO STREAM TARGET — `Projects::TasksController` replaces exactly
  this element on every add/status-change/delete, so the rest of the page (the build log, the Files
  panel with its thumbnail polling, the sheets) is never re-rendered and never re-fetched. Rename it
  and the controller's `turbo_stream.replace` silently matches nothing: the request succeeds, the
  response is discarded, and the list simply stops updating. SINCE SLICE 6 AN EMPTY TASKS PANEL IS
  ON THE MAKER'S PAGE ONLY THROUGH ?add=tasks ("+ Add panel"), which renders this whole section with
  its form, so the first add always has this element to land on.

  THE HTML REDIRECT IS STILL THERE, in the controller, as the no-JS path. Everything below is plain
  form markup — nothing here depends on Turbo being loaded.
%>
<%#
  THE ONE PANEL THAT IS ALSO RENDERED WITH NO PAGE BEHIND IT (2026-09-09, the page editor).
  Projects::TasksController#render_tasks replaces this element over turbo_stream and sets
  @project/@tasks alone, so @panel_layout is nil in THAT render and this heading would lose the
  maker's own title on every add — the panel says "To do" until the first task, then flips back to
  "Tasks" with nothing going red. The layout is two columns off the row this partial already holds
  (Maker::Project#panel_layout: no query, no listing, and `folders:` defaults to none, which is
  every folder key dropped and no fixed key touched), so asking for it here keeps the two
  renderings identical — the property render_tasks' own comment exists to protect.
%>
<%#
  THE COMPOSER IS THE PANELS' ONE PATTERN (2026-11-02, the UX audit's C4): a `details.composer` in
  the head opened by "Add…", a labelled field, and a submit that names the thing ("Add task") —
  where Tasks alone had a bare inline field whose only name was its placeholder. After an add the
  stream renders it OPEN again (@task_composer_open), so a run of tasks is typed one after another
  without reopening it; a refusal renders it open with the error inside and the title kept
  (@task_draft), which is what "the composer" means for the other five panels too.
%>
<% layout = @panel_layout || @project.panel_layout %>
<section class="panel tasks-panel<%= " panel--quiet" if @tasks.empty? %>" id="project-tasks">
  <div class="panel-head">
    <h2><%= panel_title(layout, "tasks") %></h2>
    <%# `scope:` is what names the params, and it is not optional here: a model-less form_with has a
        blank object_name, and `fields_for :task` on one emits name="[task][title]" — which Rack
        parses to the literal key "[task]", so params.require(:task) 400s on every Add. Don't
        reintroduce fields_for alongside scope either; that nests to task[task][title]. The field
        states its value, so it never falls back to a @task ivar. %>
    <details class="composer task-add" <%= "open" if @task_composer_open || @task_error.present? %>>
      <summary class="btn small" aria-label="Add a task">Add…</summary>
      <%= form_with url: project_tasks_path(@project), scope: :task, method: :post, class: "task-form" do |f| %>
        <%# THE ERROR HAS TO RENDER HERE, not in the layout's flash. A turbo_stream response replaces
            this panel and nothing else, so a `redirect_to ... alert:` would set a flash no response
            ever draws — the user would type an invalid task, watch the panel redraw unchanged, and
            be told nothing. `@task_error` is set only on the turbo_stream branch; the HTML branch
            still flashes. Inside the composer since 2026-11-02, beside the field it is about. %>
        <% if @task_error.present? %>
          <p class="flash-alert task-error" role="alert"><%= @task_error %></p>
        <% end %>
        <label class="field">
          <span>Task</span>
          <%= f.text_field :title, value: @task_draft, required: true, id: "task-title-#{@project.id}" %>
        </label>
        <%= f.submit "Add task", class: "btn small" %>
      <% end %>
    </details>
  </div>
  <%= panel_intro(layout, "tasks") %>

  <%# QUIET AT ZERO (stage 4, S4-1): three headed groups reading "(0)" is not information. The
      turbo-stream target and the composer in the head are unchanged by it, so an Add that creates
      the first task replaces this element with the groups as before. %>
  <% if @tasks.any? %>
    <%# AND AN EMPTY GROUP IS SUPPRESSED TOO, WHICH THE PANEL-LEVEL GUARD ABOVE DOES NOT COVER. That
        one hides the groups only when EVERY status is empty; a project with two todos still rendered
        `DOING (0)` and `DONE (0)` under it, which is the same "(0) is not information" argument one
        level down. `next if group.empty?` rather than a filtered STATUSES list, so the ORDER stays
        the enum's — a group that fills later appears in its own place, not at the end. %>
    <% Maker::Task::STATUSES.each do |status| %>
      <% group = @tasks.select { |t| t.status == status } %>
      <% next if group.empty? %>
      <div class="tasks-group status-<%= status %>">
        <h3><%= status.capitalize %> <span class="muted">(<%= group.size %>)</span></h3>
        <%# "projects/task", FULLY QUALIFIED, not "task". A bare name resolves against the RENDERING
            CONTROLLER's view path, so this partial worked only while ProjectsController was its only
            caller — rendered from Projects::TasksController it looked for `projects/tasks/_task` and
            `application/_task` and raised MissingPartial on every turbo_stream response. %>
        <% group.each do |task| %>
          <%= render "projects/task", task: %>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

</section>
